Start with `overview` for the goals, hard requirements, coordinate system, and
palette. Then read `world` (the tile grid, digging, and the resources it yields),
`gas` carefully (the oxygen/CO2 diffusion, buoyancy, and suffocation that are the
heart of the survival puzzle), `power` (generators, wires, machines, and
brownouts), `delvers` (needs, the job priority queue, and pathfinding), `economy`
(refining, build orders, and food), `controls` (the camera, the dig and build
tools, and simulation speed), and `flow` (survival pressure, the cycle clock,
scoring, the loss state, the HUD dashboard, and the game states). Then read
`assets` — the contract for the art, effects, and audio **you must produce** — and
finally the mode spec(s) under `modes/` for the playable start(s). The specs
cross-reference each other by name — treat them as one specification. **You design
the exact layout of the starting cavern, the machine and building set beyond the
required ones, and how the colony reads on screen** within the constraints in the
specs.

## Build requirements

Deliver a complete, polished, playable game, not a tech demo. The **hard
requirements** — real graphics, no backend or credentials, the palette and type, a
`README.md`, and the game states — are stated in full in `specs/overview.md` (and
the files it points to). That specification is authoritative; build to it rather
than to a summary here. Three things bear restating, because the harness depends on
them:

- **The fixed build/serve interface.** The project stays a **Node project** built
  with only Node.js and npm-installed dependencies. Extend the provided
  `package.json` at `{{workspace}}` and **commit a `package-lock.json`** (run
  `npm install` after you change dependencies so the lockfile matches). `npm ci`
  then `npm run build` must produce the static site — with no other manual step —
  into one of `dist/`, `build/`, or `out/` at the project root, with an
  `index.html` at the root of that directory. Serving that directory as-is must run
  the game, and it must work **at any base path** (it is played back from a per-run
  sub-path, so any URL the build constructs at runtime must be page-relative — never
  a leading `/`; for a bundler, set a relative base such as `base: './'`).
  `specs/overview.md` states this in full; follow it exactly.
- **You PRODUCE the art, effects, and audio.** There is **no** pre-made game art in
  this project. Instead, the run image puts six asset-generation tools on your
  `PATH` — **`draw`** and **`draw-sheet`** (sprites and animated sprite sheets),
  **`particle-2d`** (particle systems), and **`sfx-synth`**, **`sfx-sample`**, and
  **`music`** (sound and music) — and you must **produce every asset the game plays
  with these tools during this build**, then wire the produced files into the game.
  Run each tool's `--help` to learn its operations. **`specs/assets.md` is the
  authoritative contract**: it enumerates every sprite, animation, particle system,
  and sound the game needs, names which tool produces each and where it lands under
  `assets/`, and states how each is wired into the build — including that the
  particle systems are played live through the **`@test-cabinet/particle-runtime`**
  library already installed as a dependency of your project (import it like any
  other dependency; do not fetch or reimplement it). Load every produced file with
  page-relative URLs so it resolves from the served sub-path.
- **The reference screenshots.** The `reference/` folder under `{{workspace}}` holds
  screenshots that are visual targets for the title, gameplay, and colony-lost
  screens — match their layout, palette, and type, but build the screens from the
  specification rather than copying the images.

## Verify before you finish

Your project has **Playwright** and a headless **Chromium** installed as project
dependencies, plus network access — use them to verify your build, do not just
assume it works. Before you consider the task complete:

- Produce the **static production build** and serve it locally, then open it in
  Chromium with Playwright and confirm it **loads with no console errors** and no
  uncaught exceptions.
- **Drive the game** — pan the colony, queue digs and watch a delver mine them,
  refine ore and build walls/floors/ladders, place and power an oxygen diffuser,
  build and harvest a fungus farm, then let the starting air run low to reach the
  loss state — and **screenshot** the title, in-colony, and colony-lost screens.
  Compare each against the matching image in `reference/` and against the specs, and
  fix what does not match before finishing.
- **Capture the proof of implementation** exactly as `specs/proof.md` specifies:
  the screenshots and the two short clips, each written to the precise `proof/`
  path that spec names. These are required deliverables of the finished build, not
  a private check. Do not invent a different folder or filename, and do not skip
  the `.webm` clips.

Use the project's own Playwright: import it from your project (for example
`import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the project-local CLI with
`npx playwright`. There is **no** global Playwright on `PATH` — it lives in your
project's `node_modules`, like any other dependency. Launch Chromium with the
`--no-sandbox` argument — this container has no Chromium sandbox, so a default
launch will fail.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a **git repository** — it was initialized before you started,
with the starter files in its first commit. Your finished implementation is
published directly from this repository's commits, so work you leave uncommitted is
**not** published. When the build is complete and verified, commit everything: run
`git add -A` then `git commit`, and confirm `git status` reports a clean working
tree with nothing uncommitted or untracked. A `.gitignore` already excludes
`node_modules/` and the build output (`dist/`/`build/`/`out/`), so a clean tree
does not mean committing those — but it does mean committing your source, your
`package-lock.json`, your `README.md`, the **produced assets under `assets/`**, and
the `proof/` artifacts.
